---
title: "Callout kinds"
---

There are three callout kinds, and each is carried by a 2px left rule in its own colour
plus the kind word above the body. There is no box, no icon and no tint: the rule and the
word are the whole affordance.

::: {.callout-note}
A note, for an aside the reader can take or leave.
:::

::: {.callout-tip}
A tip worth surfacing.
:::

::: {.callout-warning}
A warning the reader should not miss.
:::

The kind word is the tool speaking, so it takes the machine voice. A title is not.

::: {.callout-note title="An authored title"}
A `title=` makes the callout's own heading, and it stays in the author's voice — upright
serif, not tracked uppercase — because those are the author's own words.
:::

A callout can fold, and the summary is the same split: the author's title if they wrote
one, the kind word if they did not.

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Folded, with an authored summary"}
The body is behind a disclosure the reader opens.
:::
